 ## Overview
-SDF Smooth Union combines two or more Signed Distance Functions (SDFs) into a single smooth SDF, avoiding sharp edges at primitive intersections [S2, unofficial][S3, unofficial]. It approximates the boolean union operation using a blending parameter $k$ [S1, unofficial]. It sets itself apart from [Multi-Material Distance Fields](1943-multi-material-distance-fields.md) by focusing on geometric distance blending rather than material interpolation, though it can be extended to blend materials alongside distances [S4, unofficial].
+SDF Smooth Union is a method to combine two Signed Distance Functions (SDFs) into a single smooth SDF, avoiding sharp edges at the intersection of primitives [S2, unofficial][S3, unofficial]. It provides a smooth approximation of the boolean union operation, controlled by a blending parameter $k$ [S1, unofficial]. Unlike the standard boolean union which creates sharp corners, this operator creates organic, metaball-like merges between primitives without branching or trigonometry [S5, unofficial]. It is distinguished from generic smooth minimums by its specific formulation (polynomial or LogSumExp) which aims to preserve Lipschitz continuity under orthogonal gradient conditions, preventing ray marching artifacts [Parent].
 
 ## How it works
-The method evaluates individual SDFs at a query point and blends the resulting distances to produce a unified field [S3, unofficial].
+**Inputs and Outputs:**
+*   **Inputs:** Two scalar distance values $a$ and $b$ (the SDF values at a query point for two primitives), and a blending radius parameter $k$ (or $r$).
+*   **Outputs:** A single scalar distance value representing the smooth union.
 
